#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059730 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059543} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68abb4f0-c532-5cb6-882c-dacfe6a945dc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Dot Product Using the General Formula"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059731 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059544} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bff08bf-0035-5f05-9607-493b301e8717" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given Two Perpendicular Vectors"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059847 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059452} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "979859e4-b60c-548d-8da6-b180400e1b07" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a 3x3 Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059848 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059453} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059849 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059454} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab642ad6-d9e0-5734-83b4-81627b9132eb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Determinant"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060920 :tutorial/content "Given two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ we know that the dot product can be computed using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cdot |\\,\\mathbf{b}\\,| \\cdot \\cos\\theta,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta$ is the angle formed between the vectors when their tails are placed at the same point.\n\nHowever, there is another formula for the dot product that we can use if the two vectors are given in component form. If the components of our two vectors are\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = \\langle {\\color{red}a_x},{\\color{blue}a_y}, a_z \\rangle,\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{b} = \\langle {\\color{red}b_x},{\\color{blue}b_y}, b_z \\rangle,\n$$\n\nthen the dot product can be computed by summing up the products of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = {\\color{red}a_x} \\cdot {\\color{red}b_x} + {\\color{blue}a_y} \\cdot  {\\color{blue}b_y} + a_z \\cdot b_z\n$$\n\nFor example, if we have two vectors \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = \\langle {\\color{red}3},{\\color{blue}-5},2 \\rangle,\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{b} = \\langle {\\color{red}4},{\\color{blue}6},7 \\rangle,\n$$\n\nThen we can find the dot product of $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =3\\cdot 4+(-5)\\cdot 6+2\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =12-30+14 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The procedure for computing the dot product works for vectors of any number of components. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨3,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨4,6⟩ & =3\\cdot 4+(-5)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =12-30 \\\\  & =-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨3,-5,2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨4,6,7,9⟩ & =3\\cdot 4+(-5)\\cdot 6+2\\cdot 7+1\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =12-30+14+9 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "37a1375e-9afc-506b-82c9-6e4d8de7588d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2933 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060921 :tutorial/content "To understand the reasoning behind the formula for the dot product of two vectors, it's helpful to write the two vectors in terms of their elementary unit vectors and carry out the computation the long way.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to find the dot product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}$ for $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle 3,-5 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 4,6 \\rangle.$\n\nExpressing the vectors in terms of their elementary unit vectors, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=3i-5j,\\,b=4i+6j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using properties of the dot product we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =(3i+(-5)j)\\cdot (4i+6j) \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 4)i\\cdot i+(3\\cdot 6)i\\cdot j+(-5\\cdot 4)j\\cdot i+(-5\\cdot 6)j\\cdot j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j}$ are perpendicular, meaning the angle between them is $\\theta=90^\\circ$ and consequently their dot product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}i\\cdot j & =|\\,i\\,|\\cdot |\\,j\\,|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{90}^{∘} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since the angle between a vector and itself is $\\theta=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}i\\cdot i & =|\\,i\\,|\\cdot |\\,i\\,|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{0}^{∘} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}j\\cdot j & =|\\,j\\,|\\cdot |\\,j\\,|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{0}^{∘} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}i\\cdot j & =j\\cdot i=0, \\\\ i\\cdot i & =j\\cdot j=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand our computation reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =(3\\cdot 4)\\overbrace{i\\cdot i}^{=1}+(3\\cdot 6)\\overbrace{i\\cdot j}^{=0}+(-5\\cdot 4)\\overbrace{j\\cdot i}^{=0}+(-5\\cdot 6)\\overbrace{j\\cdot j}^{=1} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 4)\\cdot 1+(3\\cdot 6)\\cdot 0+(-5\\cdot 4)\\cdot 0+(-5\\cdot 6)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 4)+(-5\\cdot 6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =⟨3,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨4,6⟩ \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 4)+(-5\\cdot 6).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bf1140a6-7c40-50fe-8a32-73c6ab4e8431" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2935 :tutorial/title "Intuition Behind the Formula"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186061006 :tutorial/content "How do we find the determinant of a $3\\times3$ matrix such as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nOne way to do this is to take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{7}} & {\\color{red}{1}} & {\\color{red}{-3}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{blue}{2}} & {\\color{blue}{4}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{5}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{-1}}\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{red}{7}}\\hspace{0.167em})}_{{\\color{red}{{a}_{11}}}}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{2}} & {\\color{blue}{4}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{-1}}\\end{vmatrix}}^{{\\color{blue}{{M}_{11}}}}-\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{red}{1\\hspace{0.167em}}})}_{{\\color{red}{{a}_{12}}}}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{blue}{4}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{5}} & {\\color{blue}{-1}}\\end{vmatrix}}^{{\\color{blue}{{M}_{12}}}}+\\underbrace{({\\color{red}{-3}})}_{{\\color{red}{{a}_{13}}}}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{blue}{2}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{5}} & {\\color{blue}{0}}\\end{vmatrix}}^{{\\color{blue}{{M}_{13}}}} \\\\  & =7(-2-0)-1(-3-20)-3(0-10) \\\\  & =-14+23+30 \\\\  & =39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the above, we have denoted $\\color{blue}M_{1j}$ as the minor of the entry ${\\color{red}a_{1j}}.$\n\nIn general, the determinant of a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = {\\color{red}a_{11}}{\\color{blue}M_{11}} - {\\color{red}a_{12}}{\\color{blue}M_{12}} + {\\color{red}a_{13}}{\\color{blue}M_{13}}.\n$$\n\nThat is, if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i\\end{bmatrix},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i\\end{vmatrix} & =a\\begin{vmatrix}e & f \\\\ h & i\\end{vmatrix}-b\\begin{vmatrix}d & f \\\\ g & i\\end{vmatrix}+c\\begin{vmatrix}d & e \\\\ g & h\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =a(ei-fh)-b(di-fg)+c(dh-eg).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important:** Do not forget about the *minus sign* in front of the *second term* in the formula!" :tutorial/id #uuid "99828b7b-b501-5509-8853-d17316824f5f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9175 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186061007 :tutorial/content "If the determinant of a square matrix $A$ is equal to zero, then we say that $A$ is a **singular** matrix.\n\nIf a matrix is singular, then it does not have a multiplicative inverse." :tutorial/id #uuid "2dc6054b-777f-5106-9e65-71bede9f6b49" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9176 :tutorial/title "Singular Matrices"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186061008 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the $3\\times 3$ matrix\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ k & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nthat has an unknown constant $k$ as one of the entries, and we are given that $A$ is singular. How do we find all the possible values of $k?$\n\nOne way is to use the formula for the determinant of a $3 \\times 3$ matrix to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k,$ and then make an equation with the given determinant to solve for $k.$\n\nSo first, let's use the formula for the determinant on our matrix to get an expression in terms of $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ k & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}k & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}k & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1-2)-2(k-0)+3(2k-0) \\\\  & =-3-2k+6k \\\\  & =4k-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we are told that $\\det(A) =0,$ let's equate the above expression to $0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ k & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ 4k-3 & =0 \\\\ 4k & =3 \\\\ k & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only possible value for $k$ is $\\dfrac{3}{4}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "55643210-75d5-50e5-a63b-30b951b8a201" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9177 :tutorial/title "Solving for a Variable"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064245 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064099} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abe88143-989a-5967-bb08-16804e31d948" :knowledge-point/title "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Indefinite Integral Containing an Exponential Expression"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064246 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064100} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc6023d1-1461-50d1-b939-7e08f2878df4" :knowledge-point/title "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Indefinite Integral Containing a Trigonometric Expression"}]}
